-- title:
--   Inverse image of ideal sheaves is multiplicative
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes (in a fixed universe), let $I$ and $J$ be quasi-coherent sheaves of ideals on $Y$, given as elements of `Y.IdealSheafData`, i.e. by the datum of an ideal of $\Gamma(Y,U)$ for every affine open $U\subseteq Y$, compatibly with localisation, and let $f\colon X\to Y$ be an arbitrary morphism of schemes. The assertion is the equality of quasi-coherent ideal sheaves on $X$
--   $$(I\cdot J).\mathrm{comap}\,f \;=\; (I.\mathrm{comap}\,f)\cdot(J.\mathrm{comap}\,f),$$
--   where the product is the product of quasi-coherent ideal sheaves and `comap` is the inverse image ideal sheaf along $f$, i.e. the ideal sheaf on $X$ cutting out the base change along $f$ of the closed subscheme defined by the given ideal sheaf, equivalently the ideal sheaf generated by the pull-backs of local sections. No hypothesis whatsoever is imposed on $f$; in particular no flatness, affineness or finiteness is required.
--
--   This is the standard compatibility of inverse image ideal sheaves with products, $f^{-1}(\mathcal I\mathcal J)\cdot\mathcal O_X=(f^{-1}\mathcal I\cdot\mathcal O_X)(f^{-1}\mathcal J\cdot\mathcal O_X)$, the sheaf-theoretic form of the fact that extension of ideals along a ring homomorphism is multiplicative. It is used throughout the theory of relative effective Cartier divisors on curves over a base, for instance to compare pull-backs of divisors along base change with products of the ideal sheaves of their constituent points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_comap_mul.lean

import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheaf.Functorial

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry

theorem AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.comap_mul {X Y : Scheme.{u}}
    (I J : Y.IdealSheafData) (f : X ⟶ Y) :
    (I * J).comap f = I.comap f * J.comap f := by sorry
